WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.040
Drop a slice of raw potato into hydrogen peroxide, and it fizzes.

2
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.080
Something in the potato does the work, so why is it never used up?

3
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.300
Hydrogen peroxide breaks down alone, but slowly.

4
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.180
The bubbles are oxygen gas.

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:26.813
The worker is catalase, an enzyme found in potato cells, liver cells and many

6
00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:33.513
others. An enzyme is a molecule, almost always a protein, that speeds up a specific

7
00:00:33.513 --> 00:00:34.513
reaction.

8
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:36.803
Follow one catalase.

9
00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:41.663
Its substrate, the molecule it acts on, is hydrogen peroxide.

10
00:00:42.963 --> 00:00:47.203
The substrate drifts into the active site, a small pocket on the enzyme.

11
00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:51.243
There it is turned into products, water and oxygen.

12
00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:55.223
The products leave, and the enzyme is unchanged.

13
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.927
Then it takes in the next one, and the next.

14
00:01:00.107 --> 00:01:02.227
The same enzyme works again and again.

15
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:08.367
That makes it a catalyst, a substance that speeds up a reaction without being used

16
00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:09.367
up.

17
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:12.507
Why does this pocket take only hydrogen peroxide?

18
00:01:13.727 --> 00:01:17.787
The active site's shape and electric charges fit only certain molecules.

19
00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:23.487
So each enzyme usually speeds up one reaction, or a few similar ones.

20
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:27.137
And why does fitting make the reaction faster?

21
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:33.077
Picture a ball that must be pushed over a hill before it rolls down the other side.

22
00:01:34.357 --> 00:01:39.337
Activation energy is the energy needed to get a reaction started, like that hill.

23
00:01:40.577 --> 00:01:42.517
An enzyme does not add energy.

24
00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:44.877
It lowers the hill.

25
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.970
With a lower hill, many more molecules cross each second.

26
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:54.030
The hill is a graph of energy, not a real place.

27
00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:58.337
Now pull back to eight active sites, one per block.

28
00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:01.957
Two things set how fast the tube makes products.

29
00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:06.817
First, how many active sites are working, still in the shape that fits.

30
00:02:08.277 --> 00:02:11.757
Second, the speed of each site: how much product it makes per second.

31
00:02:12.817 --> 00:02:17.777
The rate is the two multiplied together: working sites times the speed of each site.

32
00:02:18.977 --> 00:02:22.337
A site makes product only while a substrate sits in it.

33
00:02:23.277 --> 00:02:27.877
So temperature, pH and the amount of substrate each turn one dial, or both.

34
00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:33.990
Suppose one active site is bent out of shape, so the substrate no longer fits.

35
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.430
What happens to the counter, and to the rate?

36
00:02:43.917 --> 00:02:45.897
The counter drops to seven of eight.

37
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:50.437
That site is not working, so the rate bar shrinks by an eighth.

38
00:02:51.697 --> 00:02:54.777
The other seven sites run just as fast as before.

39
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.560
A site bent so far that the substrate no longer fits does not work slowly.

40
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.100
It does not work at all.

41
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:07.660
Anything that bends active sites out of shape lowers the count.
